Luvie @LUVIE_VERSION@

A pattern-based MIDI sequencer: https://github.com/benbriedis/luvie

The installer has put both halves of Luvie in place. Nothing further is needed to run it.

  luvie.exe                      the standalone application, here beside this file
  %APPDATA%\LV2\luvie.lv2        the LV2 plug-in bundle
  LICENSES\                      Apache-2.0, and the licences of the libraries built in

Uninstall from Settings > Apps, or from the Start menu entry, which removes both.


The plug-in
-----------

%APPDATA%\LV2 is the per-user plug-in directory Reaper, Ardour and Carla all read, so
Luvie appears among your plug-ins once the host rescans. If it does not, check that

    %APPDATA%\LV2\luvie.lv2

exists and holds luvie_dsp.dll, luvie_ui.dll and manifest.ttl, then rescan again.

Note that Luvie has MIDI outputs and no audio ports, so a host that groups plug-ins by
type may file it under "Other" rather than with the instruments.


"Windows protected your PC"
---------------------------

Luvie is not code-signed yet, so SmartScreen warns about the installer the first time it
is run. Click "More info" and then "Run anyway". Signing is what removes that warning, and
nothing about the download can substitute for it.


MIDI and JACK
-------------

Luvie drives MIDI hardware and software instruments through the native Windows MIDI API
with no extra setup. JACK is optional: if it is installed, Luvie finds it at runtime and
offers JACK transport and JACK MIDI ports as well.
